_⚠️ Potential issue_

**Initialize missing cache fields**

The initialization must include the missing cache fields declared in the struct.


```diff
// Initialize caches
self.accountCache = Cache.init(allocator, B160, Account);
+self.contractCache = Cache.init(allocator, B160, []u8);
+self.storageCache = Cache.init(allocator, StorageKey, []u8);

// Initialize fork caches
self.forkAccountCache = Cache.init(allocator, B160, Account);
+self.forkContractCache = Cache.init(allocator, B160, []u8);
+self.forkStorageCache = Cache.init(allocator, StorageKey, []u8);
```

You'll also need to define a `StorageKey` struct to hold both the address and key:

```zig
const StorageKey = struct {
    address: B160,
    key: B256,

    pub fn eql(a: StorageKey, b: StorageKey) bool {
        return std.mem.eql(u8, &a.address.bytes, &b.address.bytes) and 
               std.mem.eql(u8, &a.key.bytes, &b.key.bytes);
    }

    pub fn hash(self: StorageKey) u64 {
        var hasher = std.hash.Wyhash.init(0);
        std.hash.autoHash(&hasher, self.address.bytes);
        std.hash.autoHash(&hasher, self.key.bytes);
        return hasher.final();
    }
};
```

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
In src/StateManager/StateManager.zig around lines 247 to 250, the code
initializes some cache fields but misses others declared in the struct. To fix
this, initialize all missing cache fields similarly to the existing ones.
Additionally, define a StorageKey struct that contains both address (B160) and
key (B256) fields, and implement eql and hash functions for it as described,
using std.mem.eql for equality and std.hash.Wyhash for hashing.
```

</details>

