@deluca-mike You're absolutely right! For test purposes, a simple difference is all you need, and the edge cases are extremely unlikely. Your idea to use unchecked blocks is elegant - here's how it might look:

```solidity
function _testVerifyWithIncorrectRoot(Sample storage sample_) internal {
    bytes32 originalRoot = sample_.root;

    unchecked {
        sample_.root = bytes32(uint256(sample_.root) + 1);
    }

    vm.expectRevert(SequentialMerkleProofs.InvalidProof.selector);
    _sequentialMerkleProofs.verify(sample_.root, sample_.startingIndex, sample_.leaves, sample_.proofElements);

    unchecked {
        sample_.root = originalRoot;
    }
}
```

This ensures any overflow would just roll over as you intended. The approach works well for your testing needs!

🐰 *thoughtfully considers the elegance of unchecked blocks*


