review_comment:
Went for the `std::optional` approach which seems like a good way to do things.

IIUC there is no way to make this actually type safe? So something like
```cpp
    if (const auto errorCode = cliOptions.parse(argc, argv, mOptions)) {
        return *errorCode;
    }
```
could introduce runtime problems b/c the type system doesn't guarantee that error code is actually non-null - only the runtime will assure me this, right?

E.g. in Rust you would wrap the return value into an [`Option`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/option/) and pattern match it, so e.g.

```rust
    let parse_error: Option<int> = Some(42);

    match parse_error {
        Some(error_code) => {
            // error_code is guaranteed to exist as int by the type checker
            !exit(error_code)
        },
        None => {},
    }
```

