review_comment:
The type compatibility checking in `_are_types_compatible` method is quite basic. Consider enhancing it to handle more complex type relationships, including generics, unions, and optional types. Also, consider caching the results for better performance with frequently checked types.
```suggestion
from functools import lru_cache

class TypeAnalyzer:
    @lru_cache(maxsize=1000)
    def _are_types_compatible(self, left_type: Any, right_type: Any, op: ast.operator) -> bool:
        # Handle Union types
        if isinstance(left_type, UnionType):
            return any(self._are_types_compatible(t, right_type, op) for t in left_type.__args__)
        # Handle Optional types
        if hasattr(left_type, '__origin__') and left_type.__origin__ is Optional:
            return self._are_types_compatible(left_type.__args__[0], right_type, op)
        # ... rest of the compatibility logic ...
```

